  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 76-year-old man with CAD with prior stents and on apixaban for atrial fibrillation has developed gradually worsening iron-deficiency anemia over the past year. He has no overt gastrointestinal bleeding. An EGD and colonoscopy last year revealed only internal hemorrhoids, and he has known colonic diverticulosis. His hemoglobin has declined significantly despite no melena or hematochezia and no transfusion requirement. His CHADS-VASc is 5. Should further GI evaluation be pursued to identify a bleeding source? Is continued apixaban appropriate or should he be considered for a left atrial appendage closure (Watchman) device?",
    "recommendations": "Given the degree of anemia outweighs hemorrhoidal bleeding, repeat upper and lower endoscopy is warranted. If those studies remain nondiagnostic, proceed with video capsule endoscopy to evaluate the small bowel. Arrange a nonurgent gastroenterology referral for this work-up. Anticoagulation management - including continuation of apixaban versus Watchman placement - should be guided by the cardiology team once bleeding sources and risk-benefit considerations are clarified."
